1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:07,248
Suppose Jasur, an iOS developer in the mobile bank team, gets his task: connect the app to heeyID.

2
00:00:07,298 --> 00:00:12,264
He searches for the heeyID SDK, a package that he can add with one line.

3
00:00:12,314 --> 00:00:14,136
There is none.

4
00:00:14,186 --> 00:00:20,048
So how does a team build against heeyID with no SDK?

5
00:00:20,098 --> 00:00:24,920
Lesson 6 kept every key in the Vault of the bank, and no key reached a phone.

6
00:00:24,970 --> 00:00:32,336
This lesson shows what reaches the mobile bank instead: a contract, a guide, and a short list of things that the app may keep.

7
00:00:32,386 --> 00:00:35,000
First, why there is no SDK.

8
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:37,640
The bank's own team builds the mobile bank.

9
00:00:37,690 --> 00:00:43,808
An SDK from heeyID would add more work, and a second release train beside the app.

10
00:00:43,858 --> 00:00:50,792
No SDK is the honest choice: the contract is the product, and each team owns its own code.

11
00:00:50,842 --> 00:00:52,928
So heeyID gives three things.

12
00:00:52,978 --> 00:00:54,800
The REST API.

13
00:00:54,850 --> 00:00:57,800
The OpenAPI contract that describes it.

14
00:00:57,850 --> 00:01:00,752
And an integration guide with the rules for the app.

15
00:01:00,802 --> 00:01:06,680
The contract describes every call: the path, the request, the answer and the errors.

16
00:01:06,730 --> 00:01:18,320
There is one call for each step: start a registration, verify the code, set the password, log in, give consent, open a proofing session, set the app PIN and renew a session.

17
00:01:18,370 --> 00:01:24,752
Each request carries a correlation identifier, and after registration also the device identifier.

18
00:01:24,802 --> 00:01:33,248
Each error has one shape: a code, a reason key, a message for the customer, and whether a retry is allowed, and after how long.

19
00:01:33,298 --> 00:01:36,848
Each code names one outcome of the product, one to one.

20
00:01:36,898 --> 00:01:40,880
So Jasur's app never reads a sentence to find out what went wrong.

21
00:01:40,930 --> 00:01:43,640
It reads the code, and shows the message.

22
00:01:43,690 --> 00:01:46,400
Then come the SDKs that do exist.

23
00:01:46,450 --> 00:01:53,120
The bank adds the MyID SDK and the TAF SDK to the mobile bank.

24
00:01:53,170 --> 00:01:56,000
MyID runs as a native check inside the app.

25
00:01:56,050 --> 00:02:02,552
The backend opens the session, and the customer never leaves the app: no web view, and no web frame.

26
00:02:02,602 --> 00:02:05,600
The MyID credential never goes to the phone.

27
00:02:05,650 --> 00:02:10,712
The mobile bank also gets the push SDK of the bank, to receive the wake-up signals.

28
00:02:10,762 --> 00:02:12,584
Now the guide.

29
00:02:12,634 --> 00:02:17,288
Its most important page is a table: what the app can keep, and where.

30
00:02:17,338 --> 00:02:25,496
The device identifier, and the identifier of a running registration or login, in the storage that the Keychain or the Keystore protects.

31
00:02:25,546 --> 00:02:30,824
The refresh token, in the Keychain, or in storage that the Android Keystore protects.

32
00:02:30,874 --> 00:02:37,664
The access token and the identity summary, in memory only, until they expire or the app closes.

33
00:02:37,714 --> 00:02:39,536
The push token.

34
00:02:39,586 --> 00:02:45,752
The app PIN, only as a slow, salted hash, in the Keychain or the Keystore.

35
00:02:45,802 --> 00:02:47,624
And the language.

36
00:02:47,674 --> 00:02:50,600
Then the other column: what the app never keeps.

37
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:52,472
The password.

38
00:02:52,522 --> 00:02:54,344
The MyID credential.

39
00:02:54,394 --> 00:02:57,104
The MyID result code, after its one upload.

40
00:02:57,154 --> 00:02:59,072
The personal identification number.

41
00:02:59,122 --> 00:03:01,976
Document data, addresses and images.

42
00:03:02,026 --> 00:03:04,688
A one-time code, after Aziz types it.

43
00:03:04,738 --> 00:03:10,880
A limit that the gateway uses: the app may show a counter, but the server applies every limit.

44
00:03:10,930 --> 00:03:12,752
And biometric data.

45
00:03:12,802 --> 00:03:15,104
Now be honest about the edge of this design.

46
00:03:15,154 --> 00:03:18,056
heeyID cannot enforce these rules inside an app.

47
00:03:18,106 --> 00:03:20,888
It cannot look into the Keychain on Aziz's phone.

48
00:03:20,938 --> 00:03:25,376
So the guide gives the rules, and the API tests keep the app correct.

49
00:03:25,426 --> 00:03:29,144
The mobile bank is a client of the contract, and nothing more.

50
00:03:29,194 --> 00:03:31,016
Back to Jasur.

51
00:03:31,066 --> 00:03:39,296
In the end, his app calls the contract, keeps the device identifier in the Keychain and the access token in memory, and shows the errors by code.

52
00:03:39,346 --> 00:03:45,320
He adds the MyID and TAF SDKs, and nothing from heeyID.

53
00:03:45,370 --> 00:03:51,104
The contract lives apart from the platform code, so every team reads the same file.

54
00:03:51,154 --> 00:03:55,136
When the contract changes, that file shows the change first.

55
00:03:55,186 --> 00:04:02,848
Check yourself: Aziz types his password in the mobile bank, so may the app keep it for next time?

56
00:04:02,898 --> 00:04:07,816
No: the password is on the never list, so the app sends it once and forgets it.

57
00:04:07,866 --> 00:04:10,504
There is no heeyID SDK.

58
00:04:10,554 --> 00:04:16,024
There is the REST API, the OpenAPI contract and the integration guide.

59
00:04:16,074 --> 00:04:21,016
The MyID and TAF SDKs live in the mobile bank.

60
00:04:21,066 --> 00:04:25,768
There is a short list of what the app may keep, and a longer list of what it never keeps.

61
00:04:25,818 --> 00:04:30,208
With these foundations in place, Aziz can type his phone number for the first time.

62
00:04:30,258 --> 00:04:34,240
Lesson 8 follows that number, from the first code to level one.
